The best approach is to adopt the **`System.IO.Abstractions`** NuGet package rather than writing a custom wrapper. It's battle-tested and already covers `File.*` and `Directory.*`.

**1. Install the package:**
```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
```

**2. Register in DI:**
```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**
```csharp
public class MyService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Read(string path) => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Write(string path, string content) => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool EnsureDir(string path) => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**
```bash
dotnet add <TestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```
```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data.txt", new MockFileData("hello") }
});

var svc = new MyService(mockFs);
Assert.Equal("hello", svc.Read("/data.txt"));

svc.Write("/out.txt", "world");
Assert.Equal("world", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/out.txt"));
```

This gives you a drop-in mock for all three calls (`File.ReadAllText`, `File.WriteAllText`, `Directory.Exists`) without maintaining custom interfaces. The registration is `AddSingleton` since `FileSystem` is stateless.
